earth: a young neighbour asks for the story of your life and you talk until midnight; the next morning the pension letter shows almost nothing saved
after: color history: Red means 40% or more of the person's means over ten years (impulse, adventure, living now), and someone asks about the person's life, or a letter shows how little is put by
delay: 10-40
likelier: a life of travel, changes of job and big nights; a round birthday; a pension statement
rarer: a partner who saved for both; a steady employer with a pension
times: Common: perhaps half of people who lived for the moment reach their fifties with little put by; about 1 in 4 non-retired US adults has no retirement savings (Fed SHED)
scene: {N} has the whole kitchen laughing at the story of the motorbike in the desert. The letter from the pension office lies unopened on the counter.
scene W: {N} has had a wonderful time. {N} also knows there is nothing put by, and that others may have to carry {N} later.
scene U: {N} opens the letter and reads the figure twice. A life of brilliant stories, and almost nothing to show on paper.
scene B: Every adventure cost something, and {N} never once counted. Now the sum has arrived.
scene R: Would {N} trade a single night of it for a bigger number on that letter? Not one.
scene G: The grasshopper sang all summer. Now the leaves are turning, and {N} feels the first cold in the air.
- W | draw up a strict plan to save something every month from now on | v: security, conformity | chance: 62
- U | sit down with an adviser and learn exactly where things stand | v: security, self-direction | chance: 85
- B | turn the stories into money: a book, a podcast, paid talks | door | v: achievement, power | chance: 54
- R | laugh, tear up the letter, and book one more trip | habit | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 20
- G | accept it; a full life was the treasure, and the family will manage | v: tradition (accepting what is) | chance: 55
- W>R | join a walking club that does a cheap adventure every month | v: stimulation | chance: 82
- U>G | record the stories for the family, one each Sunday | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 82
- B>W | sell the old motorbike and settle every debt | v: security, conformity | chance: 90
- R>U | throw yourself into learning to invest, as fiercely as everything else | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 73
- G>B | move in with family, and let the household carry the costs | v: security | chance: 78
works: {N} finds a way to keep the stories coming and the bills paid.
works: The evening of stories turns into something that lasts: a book, a friendship, a new start.
fails: The money runs out before the plans can catch up.
fails: {N} promises to change, and the old habits win again within a month.

== everything rested on the one thing
tier: echo
stages: young_adult+
age: 25-110
tone: trouble
life: work, home, money
horizon: months
roles: parent, grandparent, sibling, elder
earth: the one mill, farm or family firm your whole life rested on closes, and you have never done anything else
after: color history: Green means 40% or more of the person's means over ten years (the same place, the same ways, trusting things to work out), and the one thing everything rested on fails: the family firm, the one employer, the farm, the old trade
delay: 10-40
likelier: a one-industry town; a family trade; a changing economy
rarer: a mixed economy; several skills or incomes
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 6 people tied to one employer, farm or trade see it close; about 1.5 in 100 US workers are displaced each year (BLS 2023-25)
scene: The notice is pinned to the gate of the mill where {N}, {parent} and {grandparent} all worked. After a hundred and ten years, it closes in March.
scene W: {N} gave the mill thirty years of loyal work, and believed the loyalty would be returned. The notice on the gate says otherwise.
scene U: {N} realises there was never a second plan. Thirty years, one skill, one employer, and no alternative ever worked out.
scene B: Everything was in one basket, and {N} let it stay there. Someone else saw this coming and sold out years ago.
scene R: {N} rips the notice off the gate and stands there shaking, with nobody to shout at.
scene G: The old tree has fallen. {N} grew around it like ivy, and now the ivy has nothing to hold.
- W | join the others in asking the council and the union to save it | v: security, conformity | chance: 40
- U | start learning a new trade at the college, at fifty if need be | door | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 67
- B | buy the old machines cheap and start up on your own | v: power, achievement | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 58
- R | march with the workers and block the gates on closing day | v: security, universalism | body: light | mark: defied an authority | closed: law: blocking the site; backfire: arrested | chance: 22
- G | take on a smallholding, and live from the land as the old ones did | binds | v: tradition, security | mark: stayed home | grants: plot of land | chance: 55
- W>G | help the town turn the old mill into a museum of the trade | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 52
- U>W | draw up a retraining scheme for everyone laid off, and take it to the council | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 58
- B>U | sell your long knowledge of the trade to its rivals, as a consultant | v: achievement, power | chance: 72
- R>B | grab the redundancy money and open a bar the very next week | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 48
- G>R | take the payout and the dog, and go to live by the sea | v: hedonism, self-direction | mark: moved away | chance: 80
works: {N} finds a new footing, and the loss turns slowly into a beginning.
works: The town pulls together, and something new grows where the mill stood.
fails: Months pass with no work, and the quiet in the house grows heavy.
fails: {N} clings to the old place long after there is nothing left to hold.
